WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.069 --> 00:00:07.089
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:07.089 --> 00:00:11.770
I can confidently say that this is the most
excited I’ve been for a smoke simulation

00:00:11.770 --> 00:00:14.389
paper since Wavelet Turbulence.

00:00:14.389 --> 00:00:20.640
Wavelet Turbulence is a magical algorithm
from 2008 that takes a low-quality fluid or

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:26.430
smoke simulation and increases its quality
by filling in the remaining details.

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:31.339
And here we are, 11 years later, the results
still hold up.

00:00:31.339 --> 00:00:32.390
Insanity.

00:00:32.390 --> 00:00:37.690
This is one of the best papers ever written
and has significantly contributed to my decision

00:00:37.690 --> 00:00:39.390
to pursue a research career.

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:44.780
And, this new work performs style transfer
for smoke simulations.

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:50.199
If your haven’t fallen out of your chair
yet, let me try to explain why this is amazing.

00:00:50.199 --> 00:00:55.050
Style transfer is a technique in machine learning
research where we have two input images, one

00:00:55.050 --> 00:01:00.989
for content, and one for style, and the output
is our content image reimagined with this

00:01:00.989 --> 00:01:02.039
new style.

00:01:02.039 --> 00:01:07.140
The cool part is that the content can be a
photo straight from our camera, and the style

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:11.680
can be a painting, which leads to the super
fun results that you see here.

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:17.540
An earlier paper had shown that the more sophisticated
ones can make even art curators think that

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:18.880
they are real.

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:23.570
However, doing this for smoke simulations
is a big departure from 2D style transfer,

00:01:23.570 --> 00:01:29.510
because that one takes an image, where this
works in 3D, and does not deal with images,

00:01:29.510 --> 00:01:31.159
but with density fields.

00:01:31.159 --> 00:01:37.369
A density field means a collection of numbers
that describe how dense a smoke plume is at

00:01:37.369 --> 00:01:38.950
a given spatial position.

00:01:38.950 --> 00:01:42.320
It is a physical description of a smoke plume,
if you will.

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:48.070
So how could we possibly apply artistic style
from an image to a collection of densities?

00:01:48.070 --> 00:01:50.799
This doesn’t sound possible at all.

00:01:50.799 --> 00:01:54.229
Unfortunately, the problem gets even worse.

00:01:54.229 --> 00:01:58.630
Since we typically don’t just want to look
at a still image of a smoke plume, but enjoy

00:01:58.630 --> 00:02:04.049
a physically correct simulation, not only
the density fields, but the velocity fields

00:02:04.049 --> 00:02:08.610
and the forces that animate them over time
also have to be stylized.

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:09.610
Hmm.

00:02:09.610 --> 00:02:13.480
Again, that’s either impossible, or almost
impossible to do.

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:18.940
You see, if we run a proper smoke simulation,
we’ll see what would happen in reality,

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:21.609
but that’s not stylized.

00:02:21.609 --> 00:02:26.850
However, if we stylize, we get something that
would not happen in mother nature.

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:31.750
I have spent my master’s thesis trying to
solve a problem called fluid control, which

00:02:31.750 --> 00:02:37.350
would try to coerce a smoke plume or a piece
of fluid to take a given shape.

00:02:37.350 --> 00:02:40.169
Like a bunny, or a logo with letters.

00:02:40.169 --> 00:02:43.200
You can see some footage of what I came up
with here.

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:49.310
Here, both the simulation and the controlling
force field is computed in real time on the

00:02:49.310 --> 00:02:54.199
graphics card and as you see, it can be combined
with Wavelet Turbulence.

00:02:54.199 --> 00:02:58.320
If you wish to hear more about this work,
make sure to leave a comment, but in any case,

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:03.600
I had a wonderful time working on it, if anyone
wants to pick it up, the paper and the source

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:08.720
code, and even a Blender addon version are
available in the video description.

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:13.639
In any case, in a physics simulation, we’re
trying to simulate reality, and for style

00:03:13.639 --> 00:03:17.190
transfer, we’re trying to depart from reality.

00:03:17.190 --> 00:03:22.470
The two are fundamentally incompatible, and
we have to reconcile them in a way that is

00:03:22.470 --> 00:03:25.380
somehow still believable.

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:26.410
Super challenging.

00:03:26.410 --> 00:03:31.500
However, back then when I wrote the fluid
control paper, learning-based algorithms were

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:37.350
not nearly as developed, so it turns out,
they can help us perform style transfer for

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:40.979
density fields, and also, animate them properly.

00:03:40.979 --> 00:03:46.590
Again, the problem definition is very easy,
in comes a smoke plume, we add an image for

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:52.600
style, and the style of this image is somehow
applied to the density field to get these

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:54.630
incredible effects.

00:03:54.630 --> 00:03:57.060
Just look at these marvelous results.

00:03:57.060 --> 00:04:00.000
Fire textures, starry night, you name it.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:02.760
It seems to be able to do anything!

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:07.880
One of the key ideas is that even though style
transfer is challenging on highly detailed

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:14.229
density fields, but it becomes much easier
if we first downsample the density field to

00:04:14.229 --> 00:04:19.890
a coarser version, perform the style transfer
there, and upsample this density field again

00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:21.960
with already existing techniques.

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:23.720
Rinse and repeat.

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:28.840
The paper also describes a smoothing technique
that ensures that the changes in the velocity

00:04:28.840 --> 00:04:34.830
fields that guide our density fields change
slowly over time to keep the animation believable.

00:04:34.830 --> 00:04:39.370
There are a lot more new ideas in the paper,
so make sure to have a look!

00:04:39.370 --> 00:04:44.680
It also takes a while, the computation time
is typically around 10 to 15 minutes per frame,

00:04:44.680 --> 00:04:46.000
but who cares!

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:51.420
Today, with the ingenuity of research scientists
and the power of machine learning algorithms,

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:54.270
even the impossible seems possible.

00:04:54.270 --> 00:04:57.600
If it takes 15 minutes per frame, so be it.

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:05.460
What a time to be alive!

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:43.630
Thanks for watching and for 
your generous support, and I'll see you next

00:05:43.630 --> 00:05:45.210
time!

